Participant: Participant B-01 (synthetic program coordinator)
Context: comparative discovery; Product Alpha Q3 portfolio anchors retained
Interview date: 2026-07-13

Q: What stops you from inviting a broader group?
A: B-01 said the role picker sounds precise but its consequences are invisible.
"I know who should edit work, but I do not know who can change the rules that
affect everyone." They delayed invitations until an administrator could join.

Q: What explanation would be enough?
A: A concise role comparison beside the picker: can view queue, edit assigned
work, manage rules, manage members, export data. The participant preferred a
default recommendation labelled as reversible, not a mandatory setup choice.

Q: How should the product announce a permission change?
A: A summary sent to affected members only, with the old and new capability in
plain language. Repeated reminder alerts would be unwanted once the change was
acknowledged.

Initial codes: permissions comprehension; notification fatigue.
Researcher note: This is directional discovery evidence, not a committed Beta
requirement or a measure of Alpha adoption.
